            0.12,
            0.128
          ],
          "content": "Q"
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.129,
            0.083,
            0.402,
            0.106
          ],
          "content": "GraftingBridging Science"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.069,
            0.129,
            0.28,
            0.368
          ],
          "content": "Various Krishi Vigyan Kendras (KVKs) under the Indian Council of Agricultural Research (ICAR) allow farmers to gain vocational skills in modern grafting that help them grow high-yield fruits, and boost their income by learning various marketing strategies and government programmes."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.319,
            0.11,
            0.647,
            0.245
          ],
          "content": "1. For grafting, take a healthy rooted plant (Plant A) (Fig. 11.3a) (for example, a wild rose variety) and a healthy stem piece from another plant (Plant B) of other varieties (for example, a yellow rose plant and/or a pink rose plant) (Fig. 11.3c and Fig. 11.3d)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.32,
            0.245,
            0.647,
            0.278
          ],
          "content": "2. Create a wound or a slit on a twig of Plant A (Fig. 11.3b)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.32,
            0.278,
            0.648,
            0.328
          ],
          "content": "3. Insert and fit the cutting of stem of Plant B into the slit of stem of Plant A (Fig. 11.3e)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.32,
            0.329,
            0.648,
            0.413
          ],
          "content": "4. Protect the wound or slit by using a cotton cloth or by wrapping film to avoid pests entering the graft until it heals (Fig. 11.3f). Cut the other branches of Plant A."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.319,
            0.413,
            0.647,
            0.465
          ],
          "content": "5. Water the plant regularly and observe the growth of Plant B along with Plant A."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.655,
            0.091,
            0.907,
            0.41
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.699,
                0.417,
                0.889,
                0.435
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 11.3: Steps of grafting"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_2_image_8.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.05,
            0.411,
            0.292,
            0.623
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.107,
                0.628,
                0.244,
                0.644
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 11.4: Layering"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_2_image_10.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.308,
            0.473,
            0.406,
            0.491
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Layering"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.319,
            0.494,
            0.889,
            0.546
          ],
          "content": "1. For layering, select a flexible, thin twig of a tree or a shrub, such as a lemon and bury the middle part of the twig under the soil surface (Fig. 11.4)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.318,
            0.548,
            0.889,
            0.583
          ],
          "content": "2. Water it regularly and observe the growth of new leaves on the twig buried in the soil."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.318,
            0.585,
            0.888,
            0.621
          ],
          "content": "3. After 10 – 15 days, the roots will develop from the area of the twig buried in the soil."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.318,
            0.623,
            0.889,
            0.659
          ],
          "content": "4. Once roots have developed, cut the twig from the parent plant, so that it can grow as a new plant."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.307,
            0.661,
            0.888,
            0.697
          ],
          "content": "These are some of the methods of asexual reproduction through which different varieties of plants can be propagated."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.068,
            0.685,
            0.145,
            0.737
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_2_image_18.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.703,
            0.447,
            0.722
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Bridging Science and Society"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.13,
            0.735,
            0.535,
            0.861
          ],
          "content": "Propagation of plants by the tissue culture technique has revolutionised farming practices like in banana farming. Farmers are now provided mass-produced healthy plantlets from the shoot tip (apical meristem) of several plants, which help eliminate virus-infected plants and ensures high yields. This is also an example of asexual reproduction in plants."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.551,
            0.721,
            0.866,
            0.856
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.589,
                0.86,
                0.8,
                0.876
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 11.5: Plant tissue culture"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_2_image_21.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.223,
            0.897,
            0.888,
            0.933
          ],
          "content": "You have learnt about various methods of vegetative propagation in plants. Let us examine asexual reproduction in other organisms."
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
